{"name":"Elderberry White","hex":"#eae5cf","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"elderberry","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any shrub or tree of the genus Sambucus;","The small, edible, purplish-black fruit of this plant, used in cooking and to flavor drinks, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/elderberry"},{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sambucus","blocks":[{"text":"Sambucus is a genus of between 20 and 30 species of flowering plants in the family Viburnaceae. The various species are commonly referred to as elder, with the flowers as elderflower, and the fruit as elderberry. The genus is represented by species native to temperate and subtropical climates in most parts of the world, including Eurasia, the Americas, Africa and Australia, and has been introduced to other regions of similar climates."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Sambucus-berries.jpg/330px-Sambucus-berries.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sambucus"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, e